6.6 Outcome families describe what happened
Suppose task execution can produce:

TaskCompleted, carrying a value and measurements;
TaskRejected, carrying policy reasons;
TaskCancelled, carrying cancellation metadata;
TaskFailed, carrying expected operational diagnostics.

These classes remove flag and NULL interpretation from consumers. They also separate domain outcomes from R conditions. Invalid arguments and programmer errors may still signal conditions; a designed adapter can turn selected operational conditions into TaskFailed at the execution boundary.
An outcome family alone does not define composition. It says what happened to one task, not how a sequence of tasks behaves.

6.7 Composition is a separate protocol
For every outcome pipeline, decide:

whether the first failure short-circuits;
whether independent failures accumulate;
which warnings and diagnostics survive success;
what map() does to a successful payload;
what and_then() does when the next step also returns an outcome;
how a collection of outcomes is sequenced;
where conditions become values and values become conditions.

A deployment pipeline may short-circuit because later stages depend on earlier ones. A batch evaluator may accumulate every task outcome because partial completion is useful. Both can use the same variant vocabulary while implementing different composition protocols.
Laws make the protocol reviewable. For a short-circuiting protocol, mapping identity should not alter an outcome, mapping over a failure should preserve that failure, and sequencing should not execute steps after the first terminal result. For an accumulating protocol, result order and diagnostic combination require their own laws.
The resultr package is a useful public example of S7 variants paired with short-circuiting combinators. Its design is evidence for that particular protocol, not a requirement that every domain outcome use the same one.
Its small worked trace shows why the representation matters. Begin with a Success carrying parsed input. and_then() extracts that payload for the next fallible step; another Success continues, while a Failure becomes the pipeline value. The Failure method for and_then() simply returns the failure unchanged, so later steps are not run and the original diagnostic is preserved. wrap_in_result() is the deliberate adapter that catches selected conditions and turns them into variants. The caller then unwraps or branches once at the edge rather than scattering tryCatch() and status checks through every stage.
That protocol is excellent for dependent stages, but it would be wrong for a batch importer that must retain every row’s result. The batch can use the same Success/Failure vocabulary while defining sequence() to accumulate all outcomes and diagnostics. A validation protocol may instead accumulate independent failures before constructing anything. Variant classes describe the alternatives; map, and_then, and sequence laws describe how those alternatives compose.

6.8 Interfaces are operations plus laws
An interface is the set of generics a class supports together with behavioral laws those methods satisfy. Method registration can check the first part; only tests can establish the second.
For a renderable model, a conformance suite might check that:

every published model–dialect pair has a specific method;
unsupported pairs fail through the documented fallback;
rendering is deterministic under canonical equality;
parse-after-render preserves the promised semantic equivalence;
rendering does not mutate the model.

For open extension families, publish that suite so extenders can run it. Adding a required generic changes the extension contract even when existing classes still construct successfully.

semantic nodes and their containment;
symbolic references that cross the containment tree;
the aggregate boundary that can check those references;
operations such as transform, render, inspect, and serialize;
the external format or engine whose authority still applies.

7.9 Typed state and raw content need authority rules
Preserving raw input beside typed state helps with unknown extensions and forward compatibility, but it creates two possible sources for the same field. The model must choose an authority rule.
Common policies include:
Typed wins for known fields. Rendering regenerates known fields from typed state and carries only unknown raw fields through. Mutators cannot leave a stale raw copy of a known field authoritative.
Raw snapshot is provenance only. Original bytes or parsed content are retained for diagnostics and audit but never merged back into output.
Lossless editor with synchronized patches. Edits update both a typed view and retained syntax tree through dedicated operations. This is substantially more complex because comments, order, formatting, and unknown nodes may all be meaningful to the editor.
Avoid an unspecified merge such as “typed values overwrite raw values where present.” Define collisions recursively:

how known keys are recognized at each node;
whether deleting a typed optional field removes or reveals a raw value;
where unknown keys are retained;
whether typed normalization changes raw spelling;
what happens when an extension later becomes a known field;
which version’s defaults apply.

The safest general policy for a semantic model is typed authority for known content, isolated extension bags for unknown content, and raw snapshots used only as provenance. Other policies are valid when their additional guarantees justify the synchronization machinery.
Consider a style document parsed from version 1. It contains a known layer with opacity = 0.5 and an unknown vendor key. A user changes the typed opacity to 0.8; meanwhile version 2 of the specification adopts the vendor key as a standard field with a default. Under “typed wins,” serialization must emit 0.8, preserve the still-unknown extension only in locations where it does not collide, and apply defaults according to the target version—not resurrect the raw 0.5 or silently let the old extension override the new known field. Under “raw snapshot is provenance,” none of the original parsed tree is merged at all. This scenario is why authority, collision, deletion, and version rules must be specified recursively rather than summarized as “merge raw and typed.”

7.10 Model and dialect as a compact example
Consider a small semantic query model with named relations and references, plus two dialect values. The model owns relational meaning and aggregate reference checks. The dialect owns quoting and target capabilities.
render(model, dialect) uses multiple dispatch because behavior depends on both concepts. A compiler may reject a valid model when a dialect lacks a feature, or lower it under a documented policy. parse() may preserve an unknown clause for inspection while compilation rejects that clause.
The acceptance laws can then be explicit:

supported transformations preserve model invariants;
every published model–dialect pair has method coverage;
unsupported pairs fail through a documented fallback;
render-then-parse preserves semantic equivalence;
unknown clauses survive only when preservation equivalence is promised;
an external engine or parser serves as a test oracle for emitted syntax.

The example connects all three chapters: Chapter 4 establishes what the model’s construction certifies, Chapter 5 defines the rendering operation and coverage contract, and this chapter defines authority and round-trip policy.

10.4 Playbook 3: Building a language or semantic model
Pressure. The package owns the vocabulary, so premature abstraction is easy.
Opening. You are inventing an AST, query vocabulary, compiler IR, or embedded language. Write target artifacts by hand first. Stay with opaque payloads while serialization is the only operation; add combinators when construction is painful; introduce typed nodes only when analysis or multi-target behavior pays for them.
Target-language fluency comes before class design: until the package can state which target forms are valid, idiomatic, and meaning-preserving, an AST merely formalizes guesses. Make one early representation choice explicit as well: will users construct the language through dedicated constructors, or will the package capture and interpret quoted R expressions? Constructor APIs are more verbose but keep host-language evaluation out of the grammar; quoted R can feel natural but requires a precise policy for names, calls, environments, and unsupported syntax.

Inventory actual consumers: rendering, analysis, optimization, comparison, or execution.
Sketch the smallest meaningful node family and write target artifacts by hand.
Give nodes precise local contracts; leave embedded languages opaque when no operation interprets them.
Place tree-wide name resolution and reference laws at the root.
Define passes as operations. Analysis as well as rendering is strong evidence that typed nodes carry useful meaning.
Extract smart constructors and node recipes after usage becomes repetitive.
Distinguish faithful conversion, policy-bearing adaptation or compilation, and an IR earned by several sources, targets, or analyses.
Test rendering laws, method coverage, normalization, and explicit refusal of unsupported syntax.

sqlr demonstrates a middle representation earned by several consumers.

10.4.1 A small AST progression
Start with one leaf and one composition node, then justify the hierarchy with two independent passes:
filter_expr &lt;- S7::new_class("filter_expr", abstract = TRUE)

comparison &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "comparison",
  parent = filter_expr,
  properties = list(
    op = S7::new_property(
      S7::class_character,
      validator = function(value) {
        if (length(value) != 1L || !value %in% c("==", "!=", "&lt;", "&gt;")) {
          "must be one of ==, !=, &lt;, &gt;"
        }
      }
    ),
    field = S7::class_character,
    value = S7::class_any
  )
)

all_of &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "all_of",
  parent = filter_expr,
  properties = list(
    expressions = S7::new_property(
      S7::class_list,
      validator = function(value) {
        inherits_expr &lt;- vapply(
          value,
          S7::S7_inherits,
          logical(1),
          class = filter_expr
        )
        if (!all(inherits_expr)) {
          "every element must be a filter_expr"
        }
      }
    )
  )
)

render_filter &lt;- S7::new_generic("render_filter", "x")
fields_used &lt;- S7::new_generic("fields_used", "x")

S7::method(render_filter, comparison) &lt;- function(x) {
  list(op = x@op, field = x@field, value = x@value)
}
S7::method(render_filter, all_of) &lt;- function(x) {
  list(all = lapply(x@expressions, render_filter))
}

S7::method(fields_used, comparison) &lt;- function(x) x@field
S7::method(fields_used, all_of) &lt;- function(x) {
  unique(unlist(lapply(x@expressions, fields_used)))
}
render_filter() alone could have emitted lists from constructor functions. fields_used() is the second consumer that makes node identity useful. The trap is representation deeper than analysis. Its consequence is a large class family whose only behavior is reconstructing the list it replaced.

constructing known fragments;
adding and reordering layers while preserving references;
reading documents from a changing external specification;
writing them back without casually dropping extensions;
producing at least one engine projection;
possibly adding inspection or analysis later.



11.1.1 Meanings and consumers
The stable meanings are source identity, layer identity, layer kind, source references, paint declarations, and expression payloads. Consumers include constructors, invariant-preserving transformations, serializer/parser boundaries, the external validator, and perhaps a later analysis.


11.1.2 Representative values and variants
Sketch one source, one known fill layer, one known line layer, one unknown future layer, one literal paint value, and one opaque expression. Draw containment separately from the layer-to-source reference.


11.1.3 Property inventory
For every modeled field, record shape, presence, nullability, emptiness, defaults, and wire omission. An engine default must not automatically become an R constructor default: omission may carry version-sensitive meaning.


11.1.4 Rule placement

scalar shape and bounded literal rules belong to properties;
relationships among a layer’s known fields belong to that layer;
unique ids and resolvable source references belong to the root;
expression validity and full conformance may remain with the engine;
version compatibility belongs at parsing or compilation boundaries.



11.1.5 Operations and laws
The package needs add_layer(), reorder, parse, and serialize. It must name its round-trip relation and unknown-content policy. If it later interprets expressions or supports several engines, those consumers may change the representation choice.
All three models can now be evaluated fairly.

11.2 Candidate A: property-rich native model
At the first design review, Candidate A is the natural proposal. Users will construct layers in R, the package promises invariant-preserving edits, and future interface or documentation generation could consume declared property metadata. A native model appears to put every known fact where S7 can reflect and validate it.
Known layer kinds are classes, and meaningful paint entries are S7 properties with local validators. Repeated scalar, enum, color, or expression contracts can be shared as property definitions or generated from an owned specification.

11.2.1 What it buys

constructor signatures and reflection expose known fields directly;
local edits can re-run precise property and class validators;
documentation and interfaces can project declared metadata;
differences among layer kinds remain available for dispatch;
typos in known property names fail at the native boundary.



11.2.2 What it costs

specification changes require regenerating or editing declarations;
presence and omission semantics must survive defaults and serialization;
generated code needs an explicit authority and review strategy;
unknown future fields still need a policy;
metadata may duplicate an external schema unless one source is authoritative and agreement is tested.



11.2.3 When it wins
Choose this model when users construct and edit known fields heavily, native introspection matters, several projections consume property metadata, or layer-specific operations need distinctions. The number of fields is not itself an argument against it. Generation from an owned semantic specification can make a large native surface maintainable.

11.3 Candidate B: typed known nodes plus extension bag
The next review introduces real documents from newer engine versions. Known layer kinds still drive add_layer(), reordering, and eventual analysis, but unrecognized paint keys and future layer fields must survive a read-modify- write cycle. Candidate B is plausible because it keeps nominal meaning at the operation boundary while declining to pretend that this release understands every foreign field.
Major concepts and known variants are classes, while unknown or intentionally extensible content is stored separately in a disciplined bag. Known paint entries might be properties or a validated known-key map; foreign entries remain inert.

11.3.1 What it buys

nominal identity and dispatch remain available where operations use them;
foreign keys can be preserved without claiming to understand them;
the native surface can focus on stable, high-value meanings;
version churn is localized to parsers, key tables, and merge rules.



11.3.2 What it costs

authority is split between typed and extension state;
collisions need a deterministic rule: typed value wins, extension wins, or rejection;
transformations and serializers must preserve extensions deliberately;
unknown content cannot participate in core logic without promotion;
read-modify-write cycles need tests proving extensions survive.



11.3.3 When it wins
Choose the hybrid when known concepts drive operations but round-trip fidelity across evolving foreign documents also matters. It is often a strong compromise, but the merge policy is part of the public contract.

11.4 Candidate C: validated open bag
Candidate C becomes plausible when the team asks whether native construction is truly the product. If most work is ingest, conformance checking, small patches, and pass-through serialization, translating a rapidly changing external document into dozens of classes may add ceremony without removing interpretation from any important consumer.
The document or selected nodes remain lists. A schema, generated key table, or boundary validator checks known constraints, while operations use accessors and explicit validation stages.

11.4.1 What it buys

close fidelity to an open or changing external representation;
straightforward preservation of unknown keys;
schema updates can change data tables rather than class signatures;
low translation overhead when the package mostly transports documents.



11.4.2 What it costs

nominal meaning and dispatch axes are weaker;
arbitrary list edits can bypass validity unless operations revalidate;
consumers may repeat lookups, presence checks, and interpretation;
reflection reveals list shape rather than domain intent;
aggregate errors can arrive later and with less local context.



11.4.3 When it wins
Choose the open bag when transport, validation, and pass-through are the main product and native semantic operations are few. It can be the correct stable architecture under external-schema authority.

11.6 An annotated choice
Assume the first release promises known-layer construction, invariant-preserving reorder/add operations, round-trip preservation of foreign keys, and one engine serializer. It does not interpret expressions.
A typed-known-plus-extension model is reasonable under those promises:

source and known layer identities are classes because operations validate or dispatch by kind;
ids and source references use shared property contracts;
known paint literals are checked from declared metadata;
expressions remain opaque and delegated to the external validator;
unknown fields on known layer variants are retained in an extension bag, while wholly unknown variants become an explicit unknown_layer carrying their uninterpreted source record;
the root checks unique ids and references;
named mutators use S7::set_props() so root validation follows edits;
serialization rejects collisions between typed fields and extension keys.

The list is the end of a decision chain, not a scorecard result. Start with the promised operations: they need stable layer identity and root-level reference laws, which rules out an entirely unstructured core. Next apply external evolution: foreign keys must survive even when the package cannot interpret them, which rules out a closed property-only model. Put the split where behavior changes—typed layer variants above it, inert extension data below it. Then place checks by visibility: variant-local paint rules on each known layer, id and source laws on the style root, expression conformance at the external engine. Finally choose round-trip equality: known fields compare semantically, while unknown fields require representational preservation. Each step constrains the next; none follows from class count alone.
This is contingent, not a destination every design approaches. If interface generation becomes central, more properties may move into the native model. If lossless transport becomes the product, more nodes may move toward validated bags. If expression analysis becomes a product, opaque expressions may become typed nodes.

11.6.1 One implementation slice
The following load-bearing slice was verified with S7 0.2.2 on R 4.6.1: insertion order, aggregate rejection of duplicate ids, and aggregate rejection of missing source references were exercised. It deliberately models only variant identity, aggregate laws, and the supported edit; parsing, paint validation, and serialization remain separate boundaries.
layer &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "layer",
  abstract = TRUE,
  properties = list(
    id = S7::class_character,
    source = S7::class_character,
    extensions = S7::new_property(S7::class_list, default = list())
  )
)

fill_layer &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "fill_layer",
  parent = layer,
  properties = list(
    paint = S7::new_property(S7::class_list, default = list())
  )
)

line_layer &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "line_layer",
  parent = layer,
  properties = list(
    paint = S7::new_property(S7::class_list, default = list())
  )
)

unknown_layer &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "unknown_layer",
  parent = layer,
  properties = list(raw = S7::class_list)
)

layer_list &lt;- S7::new_property(
  class = S7::class_list,
  default = list(),
  validator = function(value) {
    valid &lt;- vapply(
      value,
      S7::S7_inherits,
      logical(1),
      class = layer
    )
    if (!all(valid)) {
      "every element must inherit from layer"
    }
  }
)

style &lt;- S7::new_class(
  "style",
  properties = list(
    sources = S7::new_property(S7::class_list, default = list()),
    layers = layer_list,
    extensions = S7::new_property(S7::class_list, default = list())
  ),
  validator = function(self) {
    ids &lt;- vapply(self@layers, function(x) x@id, character(1))
    refs &lt;- vapply(self@layers, function(x) x@source, character(1))
    messages &lt;- character()

    if (anyDuplicated(ids)) {
      messages &lt;- c(messages, "layer ids must be unique")
    }

    missing_refs &lt;- setdiff(refs, names(self@sources))
    if (length(missing_refs)) {
      messages &lt;- c(
        messages,
        paste("layers reference unknown sources:", paste(missing_refs, collapse = ", "))
      )
    }

    if (length(messages)) messages
  }
)

add_layer &lt;- function(x, value, before = NULL) {
  if (!S7::S7_inherits(value, layer)) {
    cli::cli_abort("{.arg value} must inherit from {.cls layer}.")
  }

  layers &lt;- x@layers
  if (is.null(before)) {
    layers &lt;- c(layers, list(value))
  } else {
    ids &lt;- vapply(layers, function(item) item@id, character(1))
    position &lt;- match(before, ids)
    if (is.na(position)) {
      cli::cli_abort("No layer with id {.val {before}} exists.")
    }
    layers &lt;- append(layers, list(value), after = position - 1L)
  }

  S7::set_props(x, layers = layers)
}
add_layer() owns the operation precondition, then performs one grouped replacement through S7::set_props(). Root validation consequently rejects a duplicate id or unresolved source after the proposed edit; the mutator does not duplicate those laws. Additional related root fields should be included in the same set_props() call rather than replaced one at a time.
The parser for an unknown layer extracts the common id and source fields needed by aggregate laws and stores the complete original record in raw. Supported edits treat that record as inert, and the serializer emits it unchanged unless a documented operation explicitly promotes it to a known variant. The layer_list contract ensures the aggregate validator never dereferences an arbitrary list element as though it were a layer.
There is an important limit to that guarantee. If a semantic class inherits from a parent with its own mutation API—most notably a data-frame or tibble base—then [, replacement methods, and ecosystem verbs become inherited side doors that may bypass subclass validation or drop the subclass. Base-type inheritance can still be honest, but “construction certifies validity” then applies only to guarded front doors; the package must document which parent operations preserve the class and where invariants are re-established.

11.8 Acceptance laws

parsing and serializing preserves unknown extensions under the chosen equality relation;
supported edits preserve untouched extensions;
duplicate layer ids and missing source references fail at the root;
typed/extension key collisions fail explicitly;
every known layer variant supports required operations;
opaque expressions pass through unchanged but are not claimed valid;
external conformance is checked after serialization;
